WEBVTT

00:00:05.386 --> 00:00:11.228
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Do you enjoy maths or does it freak you out? If the latter, you’re not alone.

00:00:13.262 --> 00:00:18.531
Maths anxiety is the negative emotional response we encounter when dealing with maths.

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:22.943
The fear is real, and it is widespread.

00:00:24.243 --> 00:00:32.493
In the UK, around 36% of all 15- to 24-year-olds reported feeling anxious about maths,

00:00:32.872 --> 00:00:36.306
compared to only 10% of people aged over 65.

00:00:39.463 --> 00:00:42.353
But some maths enthusiasts are determined to change this.

00:00:43.353 --> 00:00:47.461
They want to understand the causes of maths anxiety and,

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:56.043
just like the mathematicians they are, want to find a solution. People like Bobby Seagull.

00:00:58.597 --> 00:01:00.241
<v><b>Bobby:</b></v> Hello, my name is Bobby Seagull.

00:01:00.281 --> 00:01:02.858
I’m from East Ham in the London Borough of Newham,

00:01:02.911 --> 00:01:04.833
and I’m a part-time school maths teacher,

00:01:05.266 --> 00:01:08.416
as well as doing a doctorate in maths education at Cambridge University.

00:01:08.823 --> 00:01:12.038
I also present, er, TV programmes for the BBC.

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:16.363
I write a lot of puzzles for BBC Radio 4, I write for the <i>Financial Times</i>,

00:01:16.403 --> 00:01:19.762
and I’m an ambassador for the National Numeracy Agency,

00:01:19.969 --> 00:01:22.341
as well as being the current UK Libraries Champion.

00:01:23.821 --> 00:01:27.078
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> By anyone’s standard, Bobby has an impressive CV.

00:01:28.285 --> 00:01:31.535
He studied mathematics at Oxford and London universities

00:01:31.841 --> 00:01:35.366
and also has a postgraduate degree from the University of Cambridge.

00:01:36.973 --> 00:01:38.652
He worked in the financial sector,

00:01:40.172 --> 00:01:44.957
and as a national campaigner for improving maths literacy as a qualified maths teacher.

00:01:46.917 --> 00:01:52.302
Bobby became a social media and TV celebrity after he appeared for Cambridge University

00:01:52.613 --> 00:01:56.541
on the notoriously difficult BBC quiz show <i>University Challenge</i>,

00:01:57.274 --> 00:02:05.415
where his team reached the semi-final. Bobby’s lively personality and huge enthusiasm shone out,

00:02:05.783 --> 00:02:10.677
and this gave him an opening into a varied career: not only teaching maths,

00:02:11.015 --> 00:02:17.170
but writing, broadcasting, and campaigning to help build ‘maths confidence’.

00:02:18.343 --> 00:02:23.551
Maths comes easy to people like Bobby. After all, they’re born with it, right?

00:02:24.997 --> 00:02:28.738
<v><b>Bobby:</b></v> People often ask me, when I was born, emerging from my mother’s womb,

00:02:28.738 --> 00:02:30.997
did I come out and say, ‘I love mathematics.’

00:02:32.557 --> 00:02:34.484
I can confidently say that wasn’t the case.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:39.002
Um, but as a four- or five-year old, I did find a natural affinity with the subject.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:42.462
But actually later on, when I was about eight or nine, in primary school,

00:02:42.522 --> 00:02:47.296
that I really developed my love for the subject. But it didn’t happen in the classroom.

00:02:47.823 --> 00:02:53.484
So, I used to collect football stickers, er, like many children back in the mid-90s. I remember,

00:02:53.483 --> 00:02:55.556
there was a conversation where a friend said

00:02:55.581 --> 00:03:00.382
that a player called Matthew Le Tissier from Southampton was a better footballer than Ian Wright.

00:03:01.215 --> 00:03:05.277
And when I asked him why, he just crossed his arms and said, ‘Just because.’

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:09.662
So, people will often make assertions without any evidence.

00:03:11.029 --> 00:03:13.736
So, these sticker books actually were treasure troves of information.

00:03:13.736 --> 00:03:18.446
It contained names, ages, heights, goals scored, left foot, right foot of the players.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:24.010
I took all the data from these players and input it into a really basic version of a spreadsheet,

00:03:24.776 --> 00:03:28.347
did a quick comparison between the two players and told my friend actually,

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:33.788
Ian Wright is a more effective player on the field. And my friend said,

00:03:34.218 --> 00:03:37.968
‘Bobby, you’ve got a good point.’ It didn’t make me more popular,

00:03:38.882 --> 00:03:42.903
but it did show me the power of maths, and from that age, I was hooked.

00:03:44.396 --> 00:03:49.830
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> One thing that Bobby thinks is vital for getting children into maths is a belief in their own ability,

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:52.325
which can often depend on their teacher.

00:03:54.631 --> 00:03:56.318
<v><b>Bobby:</b></v> A good maths teacher, I believe,

00:03:56.364 --> 00:04:01.468
has confidence in their students because if a student gets something wrong,

00:04:01.848 --> 00:04:05.547
if a teacher suddenly puts their hand on their heads and go, ‘Oh God, what have you done?’,

00:04:05.848 --> 00:04:09.851
then that child is going to lose their own ability to believe in themselves.

00:04:10.471 --> 00:04:14.485
A good teacher will be someone that will encourage them, but at the same time,

00:04:14.651 --> 00:04:16.470
try to bring the subject to life.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:20.163
I know that there are many students who look at maths and think,

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:22.905
‘Oh, what’s the point of the subject? When am I going to use it?’

00:04:23.971 --> 00:04:28.661
A good teacher can actually show students actually in the real world, we do use maths.

00:04:30.501 --> 00:04:34.391
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Bobby himself has been teaching maths at a school for over five years

00:04:34.538 --> 00:04:40.104
and still teaches part-time. So how does he get his students to enjoy maths?

00:04:42.704 --> 00:04:47.493
<v><b>Bobby:</b></v> To get students to enjoy maths, one element is something called gamification,

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:52.501
where young people nowadays, they love the idea of competitions and games.

00:04:52.526 --> 00:04:56.170
And again, if they use social media apps they’ll try and get streaks on Snapchat

00:04:56.183 --> 00:04:58.180
where they respond to each other every day.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:00.241
So, if you can capture that a little bit…

00:05:00.328 --> 00:05:04.290
Again, with my students, we use apps and games where at the end of a lesson,

00:05:04.536 --> 00:05:07.511
we might have this, er, challenge where five questions,

00:05:07.745 --> 00:05:11.122
er, options A to D, students work in twos or threes.

00:05:11.408 --> 00:05:14.670
And then I’ll put the question up, and they’ve got to quickly as a team work out the answer.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:18.895
And as each answer comes up on the board, they can see their leaderboard position.

00:05:19.322 --> 00:05:22.691
So, obviously, it’s not for everyone, some students prefer a sort of lack of competition,

00:05:22.716 --> 00:05:26.117
but something like that, where you try and capture students’ outside interests,

00:05:26.117 --> 00:05:28.540
because they love games, and you bring it into the classroom.

00:05:30.413 --> 00:05:34.709
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Bobby also believes strongly that children need to understand how maths is relevant

00:05:34.743 --> 00:05:40.666
in daily life, to see the practical application of classroom maths in the real world.

00:05:42.913 --> 00:05:48.603
<v><b>Bobby:</b></v> Some people think that maths is something that you leave at the school gates at 16.

00:05:48.628 --> 00:05:52.409
And of course, again, you look at the theoretical aspects of maths like plotting graphs,

00:05:52.503 --> 00:05:54.362
perhaps you won’t use that beyond 16.

00:05:54.928 --> 00:05:58.020
But every adult will be using maths in some way.

00:05:58.281 --> 00:06:01.856
Again, whether you’re thinking about a holiday and checking your foreign exchange,

00:06:02.188 --> 00:06:05.115
or whether you’re sometimes helping children’s homework,

00:06:05.316 --> 00:06:07.742
or whether you’re going to the bank and trying to get a loan,

00:06:07.995 --> 00:06:12.401
or you’re looking at your car and seeing whether the deal you’ve got for one particular deal

00:06:12.421 --> 00:06:15.676
is better than another; you are making maths comparisons all the time.

00:06:16.809 --> 00:06:20.267
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> And Bobby is determined to show just how often we use maths,

00:06:20.987 --> 00:06:23.015
even if we don’t know we’re doing it.

00:06:29.431 --> 00:06:33.106
<v><b>Bobby:</b></v> So, here we are, in the middle of London, a high street.

00:06:33.273 --> 00:06:39.485
It’s really busy, you can see lots of cars, the iconic double-decker red bus going past us,

00:06:39.985 --> 00:06:41.356
lots of people, lots of traffic.

00:06:43.016 --> 00:06:45.668
If I went and spoke to someone here on this high street and asked them,

00:06:46.208 --> 00:06:50.527
‘Do you use maths on a day-to-day basis’, a lot of people might actually say,

00:06:51.006 --> 00:06:56.565
‘No, you know, I used maths in school for Pythagoras and trigonometry, but on a day-to-day, no.’

00:06:57.334 --> 00:06:58.615
But actually, I tell them,

00:06:59.135 --> 00:07:03.555
‘Everyone here is using maths because everyone here is travelling to get somewhere.’

00:07:04.334 --> 00:07:09.826
A lot of us go to work and many of us who go to work, commute, by taking public transport.

00:07:10.746 --> 00:07:14.447
And imagine you’ve got a job interview, and your bus normally takes 30 minutes.

00:07:14.993 --> 00:07:17.434
Again, you might think on a normal day, you might just leave 30 minutes.

00:07:17.975 --> 00:07:20.347
But actually, you might need to leave 15 minutes of waiting for the bus.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:24.681
And because we’re in busy London, lots of buses, there might be delays, breakdowns,

00:07:24.748 --> 00:07:27.683
you add, let’s say an additional 15 minutes. It takes an hour.

00:07:28.783 --> 00:07:32.876
And that conversation that we’ve had is actually practical maths in everyday use.

00:07:33.243 --> 00:07:37.899
And you might think it’s just intuition, but you are using numbers to plan your journey,

00:07:37.933 --> 00:07:39.064
like all these people here.

00:07:39.377 --> 00:07:42.299
They’ve all got somewhere to be, whether it’s lunch or going to a work meeting,

00:07:42.472 --> 00:07:43.452
or picking up some shopping.

00:07:43.699 --> 00:07:46.205
All of them are planning and using numbers as part of their journey.

00:07:46.951 --> 00:07:50.694
So, the next time someone says to you, ‘I don’t use maths’ – yes, you do.

00:07:50.848 --> 00:07:52.550
All of us use maths in everyday life.

00:07:52.796 --> 00:07:56.161
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> So, does Bobby think, provided they get the right support,

00:07:56.481 --> 00:07:58.923
that anyone can be good at maths?

00:08:00.249 --> 00:08:04.904
<v><b>Bobby:</b></v> At school, I get many students and, in fact, parents at parents’ evenings, who will tell me,

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:09.262
‘Oh, I couldn’t do maths at school and maybe my son or daughter’s picked up that maths brain.’

00:08:09.989 --> 00:08:13.424
But I always think about it, when parents are at the hospital with their newborn children,

00:08:13.858 --> 00:08:19.399
and a nurse comes around checking vitals, like, er, two legs, two feet, two arms, two eyes,

00:08:19.432 --> 00:08:21.552
they never go, oh, there’s no maths brain.

00:08:21.838 --> 00:08:23.444
We’re not born with a maths brain.

00:08:23.931 --> 00:08:26.846
I think it’s our experiences in life, in school,

00:08:27.019 --> 00:08:30.138
outside in the real world that make us think that we can or can’t do maths.

00:08:30.711 --> 00:08:36.620
And then it becomes a cycle of virtuosity, or a sort of vicious cycle where you get things right,

00:08:36.680 --> 00:08:38.460
you get encouraged, you work harder, and then obviously,

00:08:38.473 --> 00:08:42.274
you develop competence and confidence in the subject or the other way around,

00:08:42.454 --> 00:08:45.289
where you make mistakes, then you get scared of the subject, then you avoid it.

00:08:45.423 --> 00:08:49.144
And then of course, you become someone that’s not confident and competent in the subject.

00:08:51.284 --> 00:08:55.122
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Bobby has written a book called <i>The Life-Changing Magic of Numbers</i>

00:08:55.601 --> 00:08:59.022
and is also studying for a doctorate at the University of Cambridge

00:08:59.209 --> 00:09:02.480
on the topic of ‘mathematical anxiety and phobia’.

00:09:04.895 --> 00:09:09.645
But whether it’s in the academic world, in the classroom, or in the media,

00:09:10.492 --> 00:09:14.169
Bobby has made it his mission to banish the myth of the bad maths brain

00:09:14.329 --> 00:09:16.926
and ease that feeling of maths anxiety.

00:09:18.419 --> 00:09:22.911
By becoming a maths ambassador, by making the subject fun and accessible,

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:29.667
he’s proving that everyone can understand – and enjoy – the world of mathematics.